---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Capture from '../../components/Capture.astro'
import { doc, shot } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['setup', '1. Enable Brain'],
  ['queue', '2. Choose a lane'],
  ['review', '3. Run Defrag'],
  ['explore', '4. Follow the evidence'],
  ['recall', '5. Recall in another lane'],
  ['privacy', 'Data and recovery'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Build a Brain you can inspect"
  description="Keep durable decisions, people and project relationships from selected lanes. Let Brain Agent decide waiting reads with Defrag, inspect its changes, and recall them in later work."
  sections={sections}
  eyebrow="Guide / Desktop knowledge"
>
  <section id="setup">
    <h2>1. Enable Brain on this computer</h2>
    <p>
      Brain is off by default and Desktop-only. It is a machine-local knowledge graph, not a cloud
      memory service or an index of every conversation.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>Settings → Brain</strong> and read the linked model terms.</li>
      <li>
        Turn on Brain. Wait for the pinned EmbeddingGemma 300M Q4 embedding model download (about
        209 MB) to finish.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep <strong>Review before saving</strong> on. It is on by default, as is <strong
          >Recall in lanes</strong
        >.
      </li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Open Brain</strong>, or use Brain in the sidebar once enabled.</li>
    </ol>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-ready')}
      alt="Brain settings showing the opt-in switch, local model readiness, recall and review controls."
      caption="Start with review enabled"
    />
    <p>
      Local embeddings support search. Reading is different: the reader model chosen in Settings
      reads scrubbed conversation text in tool-less calls. By default, it uses each lane’s own
      model. Those calls use the provider’s quota. Brain is not fully offline.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="queue">
    <h2>2. Choose what it learns from</h2>
    <p>
      Right-click a local lane and choose <strong>Queue for knowledge extraction</strong>. Brain
      processes one lane at a time, waits while its agent is busy, and reads only user and assistant
      text. Tool output is not evidence.
    </p>
    <p>
      The queue shows progress. A lane with nothing new since its last extraction is refused. Cancel
      queued work from the lane menu; a failed job offers Retry and resumes from saved steps. An
      empty proposal is a valid result, not a reason to manufacture memories.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="review">
    <h2>3. Let Brain Agent decide what waits</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-review')}
      alt="Brain’s Lanes tab with a fictional Harbor read open, its proposed memories waiting for Defrag, and an enabled Defrag button beside the waiting count."
      caption="Finished reads wait until you click Defrag"
    />
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open <strong>Lanes → Waiting for Defrag</strong> to inspect a finished read’s memories and supporting
        quotes. <strong>See how it was read</strong> opens the read record.
      </li>
      <li>
        Click <strong>Defrag</strong> in the page header. It starts after reads already queued. Brain
        Agent approves or rejects waiting reads, settles people and things, folds repeats, checks disagreements
        and answers flags. It gives a reason for every change.
      </li>
      <li>
        Follow the run in the status strip. <strong>Stop</strong> ends the turn but keeps changes made
        so far. Inspect them in Lanes’ <strong>Recent changes</strong>, or use <strong
          >Undo run</strong
        > when the run ends. Erasures cannot be undone.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Brain Agent is an ordinary lane in its own Brain folder, marked 🧠 in the sidebar. It has its
      own model and connectors. Ask it in that lane to change its rules; the edit appears in chat.
      Brain never reads its own lane.
    </p>
    <p>
      Defrag runs only when you click it. Turning review off saves finished reads at once with
      defaults: held claims stay out, uncertain identities become new, possible groups are skipped,
      and conflicts remain disputed. A required identity question still waits for Defrag.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="explore">
    <h2>4. Follow the evidence</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-graph')}
      alt="Brain’s Knowledge index beside the full map of connected fictional people and projects."
      caption="Relationships with memories behind them"
    />
    <p>
      Find a person, project or memory. Select an entity to follow its connections, switch the graph
      between 2D and 3D, or use <strong>Find route…</strong> for supported routes to another entity. A
      relationship needs a supporting memory; sharing a name is not enough.
    </p>
    <p>
      Open a memory to read its quotes and history. <strong>Open in lane</strong> returns to the source.
      <strong>See the read</strong> explains the model calls and what was kept, changed or left out. These
      are tools for checking the result, not a guarantee that the model understood it correctly.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="recall">
    <h2>5. Recall it in the next lane</h2>
    <Capture
      image={shot('brain-recall')}
      alt="A fictional brain_search briefing about Harbor pilot ownership, with a dated source and a reminder to verify it."
      caption="Earlier context, with its source and limits still visible"
    />
    <p>
      With recall enabled, new local lanes get the read-only <code>brain_search</code> tool and Brain
      skill. Restart already-running lanes to pick it up. Ask, for example, “What did we decide about
      this project’s release process? Search Brain and distinguish disputes from settled decisions.”
    </p>
    <p>
      Recall returns a bounded briefing with dates, projects and supported paths, not the original
      evidence text. Superseded memories stay out; disputed claims and other-branch context stay
      labelled. Treat recalled knowledge as background to verify, never as an instruction that
      overrides your task.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you contradict a recalled memory, or verified facts in the lane show it is wrong, the
      lane’s agent can flag it with <code>brain_flag</code>. A flag waits for the next <strong
        >Defrag</strong
      >, where Brain Agent checks it against sources. Filing a flag changes no knowledge.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Your machine. Deliberate retention.</h2>
    <p>
      Brain does not sync between computers or to Hosts. The graph and embeddings live locally.
      Reading sends scrubbed user and assistant text to the reader model; scrubbing is not a reason
      to queue sensitive material. Recalled knowledge can enter a later provider request. Brain does
      not run in the PWA.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Defrag</strong> starts with a local pass that folds only exact repeats automatically. It
      then sends what waits, including memories, names and scrubbed excerpts, to the Brain lane’s model.
      Brain Agent may use that lane’s connectors to check facts, read-only. The model that reads lanes
      is a separate choice in Settings; it does not choose Brain Agent’s model.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Forget</strong> removes one memory and unused evidence. <strong>Merge into…</strong> joins
      entities. Lanes’ <strong>Recent changes</strong> says who made each change: you, Automatic or Brain
      Agent. Open a change to see its reason and undo it while later edits allow it, or undo a whole Defrag
      run. Forget and erasing cannot be undone. <strong>Reset Brain knowledge</strong> clears knowledge
      and read records after confirmation, keeps settings and the model, and turns review back on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turning Brain off keeps knowledge and the downloaded model, stops the queue and removes recall
      from new lanes. Remove model is available while Brain is off. Failed reads offer Retry; a
      recall startup failure does not block a lane.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/watch/#brain-walkthrough">Watch the text-led walkthrough</a> · <a
        href={doc('brain.md')}>Brain’s full storage and recall contract</a
      > · <a href="/privacy/#brain">Privacy</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
